# Project Guidelines

This project uses a modern stack: React, TypeScript, React Router v6, Shadcn UI, Tailwind CSS, and Zustand. The goal is to write clean, readable, type-safe code with an emphasis on meaningful names, simple architecture, and performance.

Key entry points and directories:

- [src/main.tsx](mdc:src/main.tsx) — application initialization
- [src/routes/index.tsx](mdc:src/routes/index.tsx) — routing (React Router v6)
- [src/App.tsx](mdc:src/App.tsx) — root component
- [src/components/ui/button.tsx](mdc:src/components/ui/button.tsx) — UI primitives (Shadcn UI)
- [src/lib/api.ts](mdc:src/lib/api.ts) — API calls
- [src/store/auth.ts](mdc:src/store/auth.ts) — Zustand stores (example)

General coding principles:

- Write complete, working code; avoid incomplete implementations and placeholders
- Use meaningful names: verbs or action phrases for functions, and nouns or entity phrases for variables
- Prefer early returns and shallow nesting (no more than 2–3 levels)
- Handle errors meaningfully; do not suppress exceptions
- Keep components pure: place side effects in hooks and business logic in stores or services

TypeScript and architecture:

- Exported functions and components must have explicit type signatures
- Avoid `any` and `unknown` unless necessary; do not use unsafe type assertions
- Place shared utilities in [src/lib](mdc:src/lib), reusable components in [src/components](mdc:src/components), and pages in [src/pages](mdc:src/pages)
- Keep application state in Zustand stores under [src/store](mdc:src/store), and local UI state in components

UI and styling:

- Use Tailwind utility classes for styling and Shadcn UI components as the foundation
- Do not reinvent UI primitives; add new ones to [src/components/ui](mdc:src/components/ui) when necessary

Routing:

- Register new pages in [src/routes/index.tsx](mdc:src/routes/index.tsx) and follow React Router v6 patterns

Performance:

- Memoize expensive computations and renders with `useMemo` and `useCallback`
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders by structuring stores and selectors appropriately

## Commit Convention (Conventional Commits)

Use the following format: `<type>(<scope>): <subject>`

- `feat`: new functionality
- `fix`: bug fix
- `docs`: documentation (README files, rules, specifications)
- `style`: changes that do not affect logic (formatting, whitespace)
- `refactor`: code changes that do not alter behavior
- `perf`: performance improvements
- `test`: tests
- `build`: build system, build tools, and configuration
- `ci`: CI/CD
- `chore`: other tasks, such as dependency updates
- `revert`: revert previous changes

Recommended scopes: `deps`, `cursor`, `ui`, `store`, `routes`, `admin`, `hooks`, `types`, and `build`.

Examples:

- `chore(deps): npm audit fix`
- `docs(cursor): add project guidelines and TS/React style rules`

Short single-line form (less conventional, but acceptable for small tasks):

- Multiple small changes may be combined in one subject line, separated by `;`.
- Example: `chore(deps): npm audit fix; docs(cursor): add Cursor rules`
- Prefer separate commits for different change types.
